OpenPāṭala Hardening Review — companion to the canonical design

The previous design is directionally right, but I would not freeze it yet. There are several places where it would become painful after ingesting millions of records.

The central correction is:

Pāṭala’s permanent truth must not be today’s JSON schema, today’s Postgres tables, today’s hashes, or today’s ontology.

The permanent truth is stable identity + original observations/artifacts + append-only semantic events + provenance + immutable historical schemas.

Everything else is a projection.

That is how you make it survivable through 10 years of schema changes, better models, database migrations, new scholarship, Greek expansion, manuscript partnerships and whatever replaces current agent infrastructure.

⸻

1. The most important distinction

There are four completely different things we have been partially conflating:

IDENTITY
"What thing are we talking about?"
CONTENT
"What exact bytes/state did we observe?"
INTERPRETATION
"What do we currently believe those observations mean?"
REPRESENTATION
"How are we exposing that belief today?"

These must be separable.

Therefore:

Stable Entity ID
    ≠
Content hash
    ≠
Object version ID
    ≠
Database primary-key semantics
    ≠
API representation

That is the single biggest future-proofing decision.

⸻

2. Do NOT make hashes your permanent IDs

I previously leaned too hard toward content-addressed identities.

That is correct for blobs.

It is wrong for scholarly entities.

A manuscript remains the same manuscript if you acquire a better photograph.

A Work remains the same Work if its title changes.

A Translation remains the same intellectual translation if you correct metadata.

A Pāṭala assertion remains addressable even if later rejected.

Use opaque entity IDs.

I would now use RFC-standard UUIDv7 internally because it is distributed, time-ordered and has much better index locality than random UUIDv4; RFC 9562 specifically recommends UUIDv7 for new time-ordered UUID use cases. 

For humans/API:

PTW_019c...
PTP_019c...
PTED_019c...
PTMS_019c...
PTTR_019c...
PTOBS_019c...
PTEVT_019c...

Prefix = convenience.

UUID = identity.

Never encode:

work title
source
tradition
SHA
date
version

inside the identity.

Those things change.

