3. Hashes become a DigestSet

Every thing that can have bytes gets:

Digest {
    algorithm: str
    value: str
    encoding: "hex"
    canonicalization: str | None
}

and:

DigestSet {
    digests: list[Digest]
}

Example:

{
  "digests": [
    {
      "algorithm": "sha256",
      "value": "...",
      "encoding": "hex",
      "canonicalization": null
    },
    {
      "algorithm": "sha512",
      "value": "...",
      "encoding": "hex",
      "canonicalization": null
    }
  ]
}

Why?

Because cryptography changes.

OCFL 1.1 explicitly requires SHA-256 or SHA-512 for content addressing and recommends SHA-512, while its fixity model supports retaining additional/legacy digests and even describes moving an existing object from one preferred digest algorithm to another without changing the historical content. 

So:

Artifact ID
PTART_019...
2026:
sha256 = A
sha512 = B
2035:
sha512 = B
future_hash = C

The Artifact ID does not change.

That is crypto agility.

⸻

4. Three different hashes

This matters enormously for Sanskrit.

A. Raw-byte hash

Hash exactly what came from the provider.

raw_sha512

No decoding.

No NFC.

No whitespace changes.

No fixing line endings.

This proves:

these are the exact bytes Pāṭala observed.

⸻

B. Canonical structured-record hash

JSON has multiple byte representations of the same object.

This:

{"x":1,"y":2}

and:

{
  "y": 2,
  "x": 1
}

represent the same object but have different byte hashes.

So don’t use:

hash(json.dumps(obj))

as a protocol.

Use an actual canonicalization specification.

RFC 8785 defines the JSON Canonicalization Scheme specifically so cryptographic hashes/signatures can be calculated reproducibly across implementations. 

Therefore:

canonical JSON
    ↓
RFC 8785 JCS
    ↓
SHA-512

for Pāṭala structured-state digests.

⸻

C. Semantic/normalized text fingerprint

Completely separate.

Example:

Unicode NFC
+ Sanskrit punctuation normalization
+ spacing normalization
+ transliteration normalization

might give:

normalized_text_fingerprint

Useful for duplicate detection.

But never confuse it with fixity.

A normalized fingerprint tells you:

these texts probably represent substantially the same textual content.

A byte digest tells you:

these bytes are literally identical.

Those statements are radically different.

⸻

5. Important Sanskrit Unicode trap

RFC 8785 deliberately does not perform Unicode normalization; string content must remain as represented. 

Good.

Do not secretly NFC-normalize before integrity hashing.

Instead:

Artifact.raw_digest

hashes original bytes.

Then explicitly derive:

NormalizedText

using:

normalizer:
    patala.sanskrit-unicode/v3

and hash that separately.

That leaves an inspectable transformation:

RAW BYTES
   │
   │ SanskritUnicodeNormalizer v3
   ▼
NORMALIZED TEXT

instead of silently modifying reality.

