32. Hashes do not prove truth

This should be written in enormous letters.

A SHA-512 digest proves:

bytes have not changed relative to the digest.

It does not prove:

those bytes are correct.

A signature proves:

this key signed this message.

It does not prove:

the message is true.

A Merkle log proves:

this event was committed into this log history.

It does not prove:

the assertion was scholarly sound.

Keep:

FIXITY
AUTHENTICITY
PROVENANCE
AUTHORITY
TRUTH

separate.

⸻

33. Our current event-chain design also needs improvement

A naive:

event.prev_hash

global chain becomes painful under concurrency.

You already had a version of exactly this race in the old registry design: reading the current tail and then appending without one atomic critical section makes competing writers capable of basing events on the same tail.

Do not solve distributed integrity with a global JSONL chain.

Use batches + Merkle checkpoints.

⸻

34. Better ledger design

Events commit normally into Postgres.

Periodically:

all uncheckpointed events
        ↓
sort deterministically
        ↓
event digest leaves
        ↓
Merkle tree
        ↓
root hash

Then:

LedgerCheckpoint {
    id
    previous_checkpoint_id?
    first_event_cursor
    last_event_cursor
    event_count
    merkle_algorithm
    merkle_root
    generated_at
    signatures[]
}

⸻

35. Why Merkle checkpoints

Sigstore’s Rekor uses an append-only transparency log whose state is represented by cryptographically verifiable Merkle trees and signed tree heads. 

You do not need to copy Rekor.

Steal the principle:

many events
→ one independently verifiable root

instead of signing every row.

⸻

36. Sign checkpoints, not everything

Something like:

Signature {
    algorithm
    key_id
    signature
    signed_digest
    signed_at
}

Algorithm-tagged.

Don’t bake:

ed25519 forever

into Pāṭala.

Allow:

signature A
signature B
future signature C

over the same root.

Old signatures remain evidence.

New algorithms can be added later.

