38. OCFL is useful, but I would not use it blindly

OCFL is designed specifically for transparent long-term preservation of versioned digital objects; its inventories track versions and content digests while prior object versions remain immutable. 

I would borrow a lot.

But wrapping each tiny HTML response in an individual OCFL object is unnecessary overhead.

Use:

Individual fetched content

Simple content-addressed blob store:

R2
/blobs/sha512/ab/cd/<digest>

with Artifact metadata in Postgres/event history.

Compound archival objects

Use OCFL-compatible packaging where it genuinely fits:

manuscript acquisition
provider dumps
dataset releases
snapshot releases
complex TEI + images + metadata packages

This gets you preservation interoperability without drowning the hot ingestion path in archive packaging.

