The biggest conceptual shift from my last design is therefore:

OpenPāṭala should not try to create a perfect permanent model of Sanskrit. It should create a perfect permanent memory of what Pāṭala observed, what it inferred from those observations, which identity each inference referred to, who changed their mind and why, and which schema/software produced each interpretation.

OpenAlex shows how powerful stable IDs, redirects, bulk snapshots and agent-friendly entity APIs can become at large scale.  OCFL shows how archival content can survive versioning and digest migration.  PROV gives us interoperable derivation semantics.  Web Annotation gives us robust source anchoring.  RFC 8785 gives us deterministic structured hashing.  Sigstore demonstrates the Merkle-checkpoint/transparency pattern.  Iceberg gives a model for massive future analytical projections with schema evolution and time travel. 

The resulting architecture is much harder than “OpenAlex for Sanskrit.” It is essentially an append-only scholarly memory system with disposable interpretations.

